<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mutapa Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mwene Mutapa state, Mutapa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mutapa Empire, also known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwene-mutapa">Mwene Mutapa</a> state, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shona">Shona</a> kingdom that emerged in the mid-fifteenth century between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limpopo">Limpopo</a> rivers, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>. Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyatsimba-mutota">Nyatsimba Mutota</a>, it controlled gold, salt and trade routes linking the interior to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> coast. Weakened by Portuguese intervention and internal division, it was later overshadowed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rozvi-empire">Rozvi Empire</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwean-plateau">Zimbabwean plateau</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mutapa-empire.jpg" alt="Mutapa Empire" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SweetCanadianMullet · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ZimbabweStatesAndTrade.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Mutapa Empire lay on the northern Zimbabwean Plateau, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limpopo">Limpopo</a> valleys. The Portuguese term Monomotapa transliterates the royal title Mwenemutapa, from mwene, lord, and mutapa, conquered land; the title came to name the kingdom itself. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shona">Shona</a> tradition links its beginnings to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a>: some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karanga">Karanga</a> migrated north, perhaps after a salt shortage, and an elephant hunter named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyatsimba-mutota">Mutota</a> became prominent, took an interest in Zambezi trade and found salt in Dande.</p><p>Early in the fifteenth century Angoche traders had opened a route along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a> to the goldfields near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khami">Khami</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karanga">Karanga</a> intermarried with the Tonga and Tavara and influenced their succession disputes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyatsimba-mutota">Mutota</a> led the militaristic Nzou Samanyanga clan to conquer Dande, built a stone enclosure, called a zimbabwe, as capital at Chitako-Changonya Hill and gave land and posts to his allies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyatsimba-mutota">Mutota</a> and his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matope">Matope</a>, a son named Nyahuma Mukombero conquered Uteve, valuable because Kilwa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a> traded with the interior through it. Tradition says Changamire I, governor of the southern province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guruuswa">Guruuswa</a>, helped him. The Tonga of the Lower Zambezi rebelled from about 1550. Portugal sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-barreto">Francisco Barreto</a> to conquer the gold, but his army reached Sena by 1571 and returned empty-handed, and his successor Vasco Homem's force was destroyed at Chikova.</p><p>After Mwenemutapa Gatsi Rusere died, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyambo-kapararidze">Nyambo Kapararidze</a> killed a Portuguese ambassador and declared war on the Portuguese. A Portuguese army, mostly of African soldiers, defeated him at Masapa in 1629 and placed Mavhura on the throne. Mavhura accepted terms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a>, including the Portuguese king's suzerainty, the expulsion of Muslims and open mines, and was baptised. In the 1680s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changamire-dombo">Changamire Dombo</a>, who had used the royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> to gain followers, defeated the Portuguese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mukombwe">Mukombwe</a>'s forces and went south to conquer Butua. Later reigns saw civil war and a capital in Chidima, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The ruler, the mambo, took the title Mwenemutapa and held court in a zimbabwe, a walled stone enclosure; later courts were of mud and wood. His wives, hundreds in number, were daughters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, divided into nine houses. The economy rested on farming and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, lent out under the kuronzera system, which acted as vassalage. Religion centred on spirits and ancestors: mhondoro mediums, said to have begun with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matope">Matope</a>, were consulted on war, succession and rain.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mutapa Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Portuguese writers of the 16th century described the armies of its successor, the <b>Mutapa</b> state, but how Great Zimbabwe’s own kings made war cannot now be reconstructed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-zimbabwe">Kingdom of Zimbabwe · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Rozvi Empire was founded in the late seventeenth century on the Zimbabwean plateau, after a military and political break within the <b>Mutapa</b> state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rozvi-empire">Rozvi Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On this ground Mellet argues that the San took part in the early foundations of the Kingdom of Mapungubwe and of the states that followed it, such as Great Zimbabwe and the <b>Mutapa Empire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/san-people">San people · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>Korekore tradition says Nyatsimba Mutota of the royal family led people north and founded the <b>Mutapa Empire</b>, and a new trade route along the Zambezi favoured Khami.</blockquote><a href="/work/great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Barwe emerged around 1500 between the <b>Mutapa</b> lands and the Kingdom of Manyika, ruled by a dynasty related to the Mutapa house.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-barwe">Kingdom of Barwe</a></li><li><blockquote>Kings drew wealth from cattle and gold, trading with the Portuguese and Swahili through the <b>Mutapa</b> lands and the coast.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-butua">Kingdom of Butua</a></li></ul></section>
